# d7-09 — objectives 7.2 and 7.3, balancing d7 after the rebuild.
workflow-optimisation|7.3|2|A team wants to know where to look first for savings in a workflow that feels slow. What should they measure?|How long work waits between the steps|How long each individual step takes|How many people touch the work overall|How often the workflow is run each week|Elapsed time is usually dominated by waiting rather than working, and that is invisible if you only measure the steps themselves.|Step duration, headcount and frequency describe the work rather than the gaps between it.
workflow-optimisation|7.3|3|An associate has automated a step and the person who did it now spends the same time checking it. What should they ask?|Whether the checking costs less than the step did|Whether the person is checking too carefully|Whether the automation can be made faster|Whether anyone else could do the checking|If checking costs what doing cost, the work has moved rather than reduced, and only that comparison reveals which happened.|Blaming the checker, speeding the automation and reassigning the check all assume the arrangement is worth keeping.
workflow-optimisation|7.3|3|A team's workflow has five steps and one consumes most of the elapsed time. Where should effort go?|Into that step, since the others cannot help much|Into the four faster steps, which are easier to change|Into all five equally, for a balanced improvement|Into whichever step the team understands best|Improving anything other than the constraint leaves total time essentially unchanged, which is what makes the dominant step the only productive target.|Ease, balance and familiarity all describe the work rather than where the time actually is.
workflow-optimisation|7.3|4|A team can make a workflow faster or more reliable, but not both. What should decide between them?|What a failure in this workflow actually costs|Which improvement is cheaper to implement|Which one users have complained about recently|Which one shows better in a progress report|Speed and reliability trade against each other, and the cost of being wrong here is what sets the exchange rate between them.|Implementation cost, recent complaints and reportability are all about the team rather than about the consequence.
cost-optimisation|7.3|3|An associate finds their workflow's cost is spread evenly across many small requests. What follows?|Volume is the driver, so unit savings will be small|The unit price should be renegotiated first|The requests should each be made shorter|The workflow should be run less frequently|When no single request dominates, the total is a volume story, and shaving each request returns very little.|Price and length attack an already-small unit cost, and reducing frequency changes the work rather than its efficiency.
approach-adjustment|7.2|3|A team's first fix for a recurring problem did not work. What should they do before trying a second?|Establish why the first fix failed|Apply the next most likely fix quickly|Apply several fixes together to save time|Accept the problem as not worth fixing|A failed fix is evidence about the cause, and reading it is what stops the second attempt being another guess.|Moving straight to the next guess wastes the information, combining fixes hides which worked, and giving up forgoes a diagnosable problem.
feedback-loops|7.2|3|An associate wants their team to improve prompts over time rather than repeatedly rediscovering fixes. What should they establish?|A place where what was learned is written down|A rule that only one person edits prompts|A monthly meeting to discuss overall prompt quality|A target for how often prompts are updated|Improvement compounds only when what was learned outlives the person who learned it, which is what a written record provides.|A single editor is a bottleneck, a meeting discusses without recording, and an update target measures activity rather than learning.
feedback-loops|7.2|4|A team's workflow has produced good output for months and nobody now checks it. What should they reinstate?|Sampled checks, sized to what an error would cost|Full checking, exactly as it was originally|No checking, since the record justifies the trust|A single check before each quarterly report|A good record justifies reducing the check rather than dropping it, and sampling keeps failures detectable at proportionate cost.|Full checking ignores what the record shows, dropping it entirely mistakes evidence that checks worked for evidence they are unneeded, and a quarterly check leaves months unexamined.
